# rig's systemd --user unit (PLAN.md section 5l).
#
# THIS UNIT DELIBERATELY HAS NO ExecStop, AND THE ABSENCE IS LOAD-BEARING.
# Do not add one. cmd/rigd/unit_test.go fails if anybody does, because a rule
# whose only enforcement is a comment is the thing this repo keeps paying for.
#
# THE SCAR, and it was paid for on this machine rather than read about.
# AgentBox's unit carried ExecStop=agentbox quit. agentbox is single-instance
# by flock and auto-spawns on first use, so `agentbox daemon` prints "already
# running" and exits 0 when one is up. systemd sees a Type=simple main process
# exit successfully, decides the service finished, and runs ExecStop - which
# killed the healthy daemon that was already serving. Enabling the unit left
# the desktop with no agentbox at all.
#
# rig has the identical shape: section 5f is a single-instance flock, section
# 5g auto-spawns on the first client call, and section 18's SIGTERM path is
# the same graceful drain a stop command would use. So the unit needs no stop
# verb, and adding one recreates a defect already paid for once here. This
# holds for `rig down` too: the lesson is not "no signal to a pid", it is no
# stop command in any form. `agentbox quit` was not a signal either.
#
# IT MANAGES production ONLY, and by construction rather than by a flag
# somebody might omit (section 37, precondition 5). It sets no
# XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and passes no --estate, so it inherits the ordinary
# environment and reaches the default runtime directory, which is
# production's. development is always placed explicitly, so this unit cannot
# reach it.
#
# STARTING IT WHILE A DAEMON IS ALREADY UP USED TO LOOP FOREVER, and the
# paragraph that stood here was wrong on both of its claims. It called this
# "one wrinkle, inherited and accepted" and said the unit reports inactive
# while the incumbent keeps serving. MEASURED 2026-09-16 against a running
# daemon: the unit reports activating (auto-restart), restarts every 2 s, and
# does not stop - 14 restarts in 28 seconds, about five journal lines each.
#
# The start rate limiter does not catch it. RestartSec=2 against the default
# StartLimitBurst=5 over StartLimitIntervalSec=10s puts the fifth restart at
# the edge of the window rather than inside it, so the limiter never fires.
# Both settings below are widened for that reason, and they bound any crash
# loop rather than only this one.
#
# THE REFUSAL IS NOT A FAULT, so it must not be retried. rigd exits
# exitAlreadyRunning (8, in cmd/rigd/main.go) when an incumbent holds the
# runtime directory or the estate name, and RestartPreventExitStatus tells
# systemd that status means "do not come back". A real crash still exits 1 and
# is still restarted, which is what Restart=on-failure is here for.
#
# WHY IT IS A THIRD NUMBER rather than 0 or 1: exiting 0 is the agentbox
# defect in the header above, where systemd read a refusal as the service
# finishing. Exiting 1 is this one. cmd/rigd/unit_test.go ties the number here
# to the constant in the Go source so the two cannot drift apart.
#
# AND THE CASE IS NOT RARE. The comment above assumed that at login nothing is
# running yet. A development estate takes the DEFAULT runtime directory unless
# XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is placed explicitly, so a development rigd left up is
# exactly the incumbent this hits - which is what was running when it was
# measured.

[Unit]
Description=rig, the estate platform
Documentation=https://github.com/boris-milner/rig

# graphical-session.target, not default.target: rig needs the session bus and
# the display for section 12's toasts and section 11's tray.
PartOf=graphical-session.target
After=graphical-session.target

# In [Unit], not [Service]. systemd moved these and still accepts the old
# placement - by printing a warning, which cmd/rigd/unit_test.go reads as a
# failure because it gates on empty output rather than on the exit code.
StartLimitIntervalSec=60
StartLimitBurst=5

[Service]
Type=simple
ExecStart=%h/.local/bin/rigd
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=2

# 8 is exitAlreadyRunning in cmd/rigd/main.go. Keep the two in step. The
# number is above the LSB range on purpose: systemd names lower ones from
# that table and would print NOTIMPLEMENTED beside a refusal that is neither.
RestartPreventExitStatus=8

[Install]
WantedBy=graphical-session.target
